  "mechanisms": [
    "The emotional response to colors in multicolored images can be predicted by a model that transforms ordinary RGB-histograms into emotion descriptors based on psychophysical experiments for single and two-color combinations (Ou et al. model).",
    "The model calculates an expectation vector for each image, representing the mean score for activity, weight, and heat factors."
  ],
  "moderators": [],
  "implementation_implications": [
    "The method can be used in image retrieval systems to sort or preselect images based on semantic properties of colors, especially when users are interested in samples with strong emotional content.",
    "The color emotion descriptors can be used for image classification and image indexing.",
    "The method is time-efficient for large image databases as it only involves transformations on RGB-histograms."
  ],
  "domains": [
    "A6_Visual_Form",
    "A9_Task_Cognition"
  ],
  "overall_theory_links": [
    "PP",
    "IC"
  ],
  "tables": [
    {
      "table_id": "Table I",
      "description": "Pearson r correlation coefficient and R² correlation between observer judgments and predicted emotion values.",
      "key_stats": [
        "Heat: r=0.97 (Mean), r=0.90 (All); R²=0.95 (Mean), R²=0.81 (All)",
        "Weight: r=0.98 (Mean), r=0.93 (All); R²=0.97 (Mean), R²=0.86 (All)",
        "Activity: r=0.86 (Mean), r=0.68 (All); R²=0.73 (Mean), R²=0.47 (All)"
      ],
      "n_findings_extracted_from": 6
    }
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Lack of control over user environment (monitor settings, illumination, background colors, psychological state) when used on public web pages.",
    "Image content (objects, faces, context) other than color can affect emotional response, potentially overriding color-based emotions.",
    "Risk of users deliberately submitting answers disagreeing with their own emotional feelings in anonymous public web tests.",
    "The activity factor is harder for observers to comprehend and apply on multicolored images, leading to more varying opinions.",
    "The selection of samples for the main study, while aiming for homogeneous coverage, might not be ideal for representing the entire target database, which tends to have more 'neutral' than 'extreme' samples."
  ],
